In conclusion, this work successfully completed the task it initially proposed, which is to study information diffusion in microblogging social networks by accurately identifying the subset of indispensable features of Bluesky, designing a mathematical model that was able to represent them, introducing restrictions to reduce and atomize complexity and scope, designing a discrete-event simulation to evaluate the model as well as distinguishing the inputs, and implementing a high performance engine in a systems level programming language to achieve scalability.
In parallel, it analyzed empirical data to inform the user behaviour in the simulation and calibrate all the parameters needed by the simulation to run, as well as extracting and sampling a subset of Bluesky follower topology which conserves the social network properties and, lastly, obtained ground truth from the empirical data to compare the synthetic cascades in the reposts power-law and structural virality.
With the combination of both the simulation and empirical data, results can be obtained and evaluated. Those are satisfactory as, despite the feature difference between the modeled simulation and Bluesky, the bulk of the distributions matches, confirming the underlying assumptions are correct. For the not matching assumptions (depth and width of the cascades), explanations are provided that inform why the work done until now is a solid basis to expand upon it with content-aware posts, more features and deeper analysis of the topology, making this work a solid first step into an accurate cascade synthetic simulator, allowing the study of hotter topics such as echo chambers, misinformation and democratic welfare in online platforms.




